{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1778874041,"nonce":"8B23C7Crb3NOjkypvUq7QfIu5AQ=","title":"Spec: Feedbax Studio structural authoring workspace","message":"## Purpose\n\nDefine Feedbax Studio's structural/scenario authoring workspace: the top pane, stage-aware graph/task/biomechanics/loss/metric/probe editing, visual lowering into durable specs, and the contract that keeps rich UI interactions aligned with Feedbax execution.\n\nThis issue is the companion to the pipeline workspace spec. The pipeline spec defines how stages and collections flow through the product; this spec defines what the user edits when a stage is selected.\n\n## Current State\n\nStudio has the start of the right primitives, but they are spread across separate stores and panels:\n\n- `GraphSpec` represents a causal model graph with nodes, wires, nested subgraphs, taps, barnacles, user ports, and UI state.\n- The top pane currently renders only the model graph canvas plus component/properties sidebars.\n- `trainingStore` owns a global `TrainingSpec`, `TaskSpec`, and composite `LossTermSpec`; loss authoring happens in the bottom training panel through forms.\n- Available loss probes are discovered from barnacles, taps, and output ports; selector strings currently include `probe:\u003cid\u003e`, `port:\u003cnode\u003e.\u003cport\u003e`, and `path:\u003cdotted.path\u003e`.\n- `AnalysisPageSpec` and `analysisStore` provide a separate analysis DAG with data-source field paths and transforms.\n- Time aggregation exists for loss terms, but it is not yet a shared temporal selector model for tasks, losses, metrics, analysis slicing, and playback.\n- Biomechanics/task structure is represented mostly through graph nodes, component params, trajectory snapshots, and task/eval parameter forms; there is not yet a dedicated workspace scene for goals, effectors, obstacles, muscles, links, or objective overlays.\n\nThe intended refactor is to make the top pane a stage-aware structural/scenario editor rather than only a causal graph editor.\n\n## Core Decisions\n\n1. The top pane edits a `ScenarioSpec` or equivalent stage-scoped structural workspace.\n2. A scenario includes model graph, task/workspace, biomechanics/plant view, objectives, metrics, probes, temporal semantics, and UI layout metadata.\n3. The visible objects in the scenario editor are projections of typed specs, not separate frontend-only objects.\n4. Losses and metrics are objective/equation-like specs over selectors, targets, time, and aggregation; they should not be forced into the causal graph model.\n5. Causal subgraphs, structured/acausal equation subgraphs, Penzai subgraphs, and reusable graph templates need different canvas/editor contracts even if they share registry discovery.\n6. Stage context determines what is editable, inherited, locked, or viewed as an artifact.\n7. Rich visual authoring should be possible later without breaking existing graph/spec/back-end execution paths.\n\n## Scenario Spec\n\nA first-class scenario should include:\n\n- Stable scenario ID, schema version, label, and provenance.\n- Stage ownership and optional parent scenario/inheritance reference.\n- Model graph spec and graph UI state.\n- Subgraph specs and subgraph-editor metadata.\n- Task/workspace spec: targets, trajectories, trial distributions, obstacles, phases/events, and task parameters.\n- Biomechanics/plant spec or view model: links, joints, muscles, end effectors, coordinate frames, units, constraints, and editable parameters.\n- Objective spec: losses, regularizers, penalties, rewards, constraints, and named metric terms.\n- Probe/intervention/selector specs.\n- Shared temporal schema: trial phases, events, selectors, schedules, playback ranges.\n- Validation state and lowering diagnostics.\n- Layout/layer state needed to reopen the same authoring view.\n\nThis should generalize the current `GraphSpec` + `TaskSpec` + `LossTermSpec` + `AnalysisPageSpec` fragments without requiring an immediate rewrite of all backends.\n\n## Canvas Layers\n\nThe top pane should become a layered structural workspace. Expected layers include:\n\n- Model graph layer: causal Feedbax components, wires, ports, taps, barnacles, composites, and subgraphs.\n- Subgraph editor layer: context-specific editors for causal subgraphs, acausal/DAE systems, Penzai modules, and template/composite nodes.\n- Biomechanics layer: plant/body visualization such as links, joints, muscles, end effectors, coordinate frames, and workspace bounds.\n- Task layer: goals, target distributions, trajectories, obstacles, cue timing, trial variants, and eval conditions.\n- Objective layer: loss/metric/probe overlays attached to graph ports, state paths, biomechanics points, task objects, artifact fields, or analysis outputs.\n- Temporal layer: event/phase markers, time windows, schedules, and playback/scrubbing controls.\n- Artifact/trajectory layer: previews of executed runs/evals/analyses when the selected pipeline stage has outputs.\n\nThese can be implemented incrementally. The architectural requirement is that every layer lowers to typed specs and shares selectors/time semantics.\n\n## Stage-Aware Behavior\n\nThe scenario editor should change with the selected pipeline stage:\n\n### Training Stage\n\nThe top pane should allow editing:\n\n- Model graph and relevant subgraphs.\n- Training task/workspace.\n- Training objective/loss terms and regularizers.\n- Probes/interventions needed for the objective or monitoring.\n- Temporal selectors and schedules used during training.\n\nLaunching training freezes a snapshot of these specs for the emitted manifest.\n\n### Evaluation Stage\n\nThe top pane should show:\n\n- The trained model reference/artifact, usually locked by default.\n- Eval task/condition structure, inheriting from the training scenario unless explicitly forked.\n- Eval metrics, probes, perturbations, trial distributions, and temporal selectors.\n- Artifact previews once evals complete.\n\nEditing eval task/metric state should not accidentally mutate the training task. The UI should expose inherited, overridden, and independent fields clearly.\n\n### Analysis Stage\n\nThe top pane may show:\n\n- Analysis graph/template and data-source bindings.\n- Selected trajectory/artifact/field structure.\n- Probe/state/task/model selectors used as analysis inputs.\n- Visual previews or artifact overlays that make analysis outputs inspectable.\n\nIt is acceptable for the existing bottom analysis panel to remain during migration, but the long-term contract should treat analysis structure as stage-owned typed specs over selected input collections.\n\n### Report Stage\n\nThe top pane may show:\n\n- Report composition and selected products.\n- Figure/table/narrative previews.\n- Provenance of selected inputs.\n\nReport authoring should not be conflated with analysis computation unless a registered analysis/report transform actually produces the report product.\n\n## Objective and Loss Authoring\n\nLoss and metric terms should become structured objective terms, not merely form rows under a training tab.\n\nA generalized `ObjectiveTermSpec` should include:\n\n- Stable term ID, type ID, label, role (`loss`, `metric`, `constraint`, `reward`, `regularizer`, etc.).\n- Source selector: graph port, probe, state path, component field, biomechanical point, task object, artifact field, or analysis output.\n- Target selector or target value: task goal, trajectory, constant, reference artifact, paired source, or generated target.\n- Operator/relationship: distance, difference, energy, norm, cross-entropy, barrier, tracking error, endpoint error, etc.\n- Penalty/norm/aggregation: squared L2, L2, L1, Huber, RMSE, custom registered objective type.\n- Temporal selector and aggregation level.\n- Weight or schedule.\n- Units, coordinate frame, and shape/dtype expectations where relevant.\n- Validation/lowering status and executable builder reference.\n\nExample UI semantics: drawing a line from the end effector to a goal should create or edit an objective term whose source is the end-effector selector, target is the goal selector, penalty is a chosen distance/norm, time selector is a chosen phase/range, and weight/schedule are explicit. The line and any flag/label are visual projections of that spec.\n\nThe current `LossTermSpec` can remain as a compatibility target for execution, but the authoring contract should be broad enough to support visual task/biomechanics-driven objectives.\n\n## Selector Contract\n\nSelector strings are a useful seed, but Studio needs typed selector references that can round-trip and validate across layers.\n\nSelectors should be able to reference:\n\n- Registered graph components and ports.\n- Taps and barnacle probes/interventions.\n- State paths and PyTree paths.\n- Component parameters or internal fields when explicitly exposed.\n- Task objects such as goals, trajectories, cue events, or trial fields.\n- Biomechanics objects such as joints, muscles, links, moment arms, end effectors, or workspace points.\n- Artifact fields, tables, trajectory arrays, model manifests, and evaluation outputs.\n- Analysis node outputs and report products.\n\nEach selector should declare enough context to validate it: namespace, target ID/path, expected shape/dtype, units, frame, stage compatibility, and whether it is editable, observed, or generated.\n\nA string representation can still be used for compact serialization, but there should be a structured canonical form underneath.\n\n## Task and Biomechanics Authoring\n\nTasks should be visually and structurally editable, not only selected as Python presets. The spec must leave room for:\n\n- Goal points and target distributions.\n- Tracking trajectories.\n- Obstacles or workspace constraints.\n- Cue timing and event schedules.\n- Trial variant sets for evals and sweeps.\n- Task inheritance from training to eval.\n\nBiomechanics/plant authoring should leave room for:\n\n- Viewing and editing link lengths, joint limits, muscles, force generators, and endpoints.\n- Selecting physical objects as objective/probe sources.\n- Visual overlays between task objects and plant objects.\n- Coordinate frames, units, and scaling metadata.\n- Future CAD-like editing without making it required for the first implementation.\n\nThe MVP can begin with readonly or parameter-driven views. It should not choose schemas that prevent richer editing later.\n\n## Subgraph and Component Taxonomy\n\nThe authoring UI must respect the registry/subgraph distinctions captured in `c6c6da0` and related graph issues:\n\n- Leaf components are executable Python-backed atoms.\n- Causal composite/template components can be serialized graph templates built from registered components.\n- Causal Feedbax subgraphs are editable nested signal-flow graphs.\n- Structured/acausal equation subgraphs are equation-system editors or templates, not ordinary causal port-flow graphs.\n- Penzai subgraphs may be opaque or use a specialized neural-module editor.\n- Analysis graphs are DAGs over artifacts/state/analysis products, not the same thing as the model execution graph.\n\nThe UI may share infrastructure, but compatibility rules, palettes, ports, validation, and lowering need to be per context.\n\n## Temporal Model\n\nThe structural editor should expose one temporal vocabulary shared by training, eval, analysis, and visualization:\n\n- Named phases/events/epochs.\n- Ranges relative to events such as target onset, go cue, movement onset, or hold onset.\n- Absolute step ranges when appropriate.\n- Repeated trial windows and per-trial alignment.\n- Schedules over time, trial, batch, epoch, or curriculum phase.\n- Aggregation levels: sample, time window, trial, batch, run, run set, evaluation set.\n\nThis is critical because task definitions, eval variants, loss terms, metrics, and analyses can otherwise disagree silently about what \"movement\", \"final\", or \"pre-go\" means.\n\n## Lowering and Validation\n\nThe design rule is strict: what is shown on screen must be the user's executable/durable intention.\n\nThe implementation should provide:\n\n- Frontend spec objects as the single source of truth for visible domain state.\n- Backend validation endpoints for scenario/objective/task/eval/analysis specs.\n- Deterministic lowering from scenario specs to current `GraphSpec`, `TaskSpec`, `TrainingSpec`, `LossTermSpec`, eval specs, analysis specs, and manifests.\n- Structured validation errors linked to selectors, temporal specs, graph nodes, task objects, objective terms, and layers.\n- Round-trip tests: spec -\u003e UI projection -\u003e edit -\u003e spec -\u003e validate/lower -\u003e execute/manifest.\n\nNo rich visual manipulation should remain only as canvas coordinates, decorations, or local React state when it affects execution or durable project meaning.\n\n## Autosave and Versioning\n\nThe authoring workspace should autosave scenario drafts and their UI state, including:\n\n- Layer visibility and viewport state.\n- Selected stage/scenario/objective/probe/task object.\n- Draft scenario specs and inheritance/override markers.\n- Last validation result.\n- Undo/redo history or mutation log where practical.\n- Links to executed spec snapshots and produced manifests.\n\nAutosave should not mutate already executed spec snapshots. Editing after a run creates a new draft/version.\n\n## Implementation Direction\n\nA plausible sequence:\n\n1. Introduce `ScenarioSpec` as a wrapper around current graph/task/loss/time/probe state.\n2. Add a typed selector model while preserving current selector strings as a compatibility serialization.\n3. Promote `LossTermSpec` editing into an objective layer/inspector, with compatibility lowering to current backend loss validation/execution.\n4. Move `TaskSpec` into stage/scenario ownership and add inheritance/fork metadata.\n5. Add a simple biomechanics/workspace view for the existing reaching task and plant components.\n6. Extend backend validation to scenario/objective/task selectors and temporal selectors.\n7. Convert `TrainingPanel` into training-stage execution/status controls, not the owner of task/loss structure.\n8. Fold analysis page specs into stage-owned analysis specs over selected input collections.\n9. Add subgraph-editor dispatch based on registered subgraph/component context.\n\nThis is a significant Studio refactor, but it can be staged without forcing a wholesale backend rewrite.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\nA first serious implementation should satisfy:\n\n- Top-pane edits to graph, task, loss/objective, probes, and temporal selectors persist in a typed scenario spec.\n- Training and eval stages can use separate scenarios with explicit inheritance/overrides.\n- Visual objective creation/editing lowers to the same objective/loss spec used by backend validation and execution.\n- Selectors can reference graph ports/probes/state paths and leave room for task/biomechanics/artifact/analysis references.\n- Validation errors are attached to visible objects and spec paths.\n- Executed runs/evals/analyses reference immutable scenario/spec snapshots.\n- The UI remains usable before Mandible integration and does not depend on hidden Mandible state.\n\n## Open Decisions for Implementation-Time Feedback\n\nThese are not blockers, but future agents should ask before making large UX commitments:\n\n- How much biomechanics editing should be in the first version versus readonly visualization plus parameter controls.\n- Whether the analysis graph should move fully into the top pane immediately or remain in the bottom pane during migration while sharing the new stage spec.\n- The exact visual style for objective overlays, flags, inspectors, and temporal controls.\n- The canonical structured selector schema and compact string syntax.\n- Whether to migrate `LossTermSpec` directly into a generalized `ObjectiveTermSpec` or keep it as the execution-lowering target under a broader authoring spec.\n- Which subgraph contexts need specialized editors first: causal composites, acausal/DAE systems, Penzai modules, or analysis graphs.\n\n## Related Issues\n\n- `93f79f2` — Feedbax Studio design coordination.\n- `8d94424` — Feedbax-wide design coordination.\n- `c6c6da0` — Feedbax manifests and provider contract.\n- `6116155` — typed subgraph taxonomy and pluggable canvas architecture.\n- `e8ec78e` — unified analysis/model graph canvas long-term direction.\n- `a6efafc` — DAE/acausal subgraphs.\n- `f22dbf4` — subgraphs in analysis DAG.\n- `4e0d925` — bottom-pane collection-transform issue.\n\n---\nCo-Authored-By: Codex (GPT-5) \u003ccodex@openai.com\u003e","files":null}]}